UART1's transmit side, for MIDI out: a TX DMA (TXCNT written: that many
bytes from TXADR) or a BUF write puts bytes on the wire, one per 31 250
baud byte time (320 us), after any still going, and the TX pending
(CON0 bit 15; its interrupt with bit 2) rises when the last has gone.
Upstream raised the pending at once and sent nothing. With the UART off
(CON0 bit 0 clear) nothing is sent; the bytes are counted. Every byte
sent is kept with the cycle its stop bit ended until the runner drains
it (Periph::midi_take_sent), so a runner can capture the MIDI output:
fwtest's --midi-out, and the F-Emulator app's MIDI output to the host
(harness/upstream/fm1-emu-midi-out.md).
The DMA reads its bytes when TXCNT is written.

Our own change (session midi-in-out, 2026-10-24), from the SDK's UART
register map and FM-1-RE's MIDI document (PROVENANCE P-41, P-45, P-46). The
harness test and suite case for MIDI out check it; on a board
with no MIDI OUT jack (the FM-1) the bytes go nowhere on the real device.

